<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyges of Lydia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 680–644 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyges was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> from around 680 BCE and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how he killed the last Heraclid king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candaules">Candaules</a>, and took his throne and queen. He attacked the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, sent rich gifts to Delphi and sought Assyrian help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, who killed him around 644 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia.jpg" alt="Gyges of Lydia" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Der_Ring_des_Gyges_(Ferrara_16_Jh).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom becomes historical with <b>Gyges</b>, who seized the throne around 680 BCE by killing the last Heraclid king, Candaules; Herodotus tells a famous tale of how Candaules’ wife made Gyges choose between death and regicide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The word tyrannos was probably borrowed: it may have come from Lydian or another language of Asia Minor, and one of its earliest uses is by the poet Archilochus, writing about <b>Gyges of Lydia</b>, who had killed King Candaules and taken his throne and his queen.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers from Miletus founded Abydos c. 680 BCE on the Asian shore of the Hellespont, at the narrows opposite Sestos, with the agreement of the Lydian king <b>Gyges</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a></li><li><blockquote>They overran Phrygia around 696 BCE, when King Midas is said to have killed himself, and later sacked Sardis, killing <b>Gyges of Lydia</b> around 644 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gyges</b> of the Kingdom of Lydia captured its lower town in the 7th century BCE, and it then passed to the Achaemenid Empire and the Delian League.</blockquote><a href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a></li><li><blockquote>Smyrna fought the Kingdom of Lydia, resisting <b>Gyges</b>, but Alyattes destroyed it c. 600 BCE, after which it survived only as villages.</blockquote><a href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.49,28.04&amp;year=-662" aria-label="Sardis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5247 782 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gyges of Lydia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 1052h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.49,28.04&amp;year=-662">What was here in 662 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our fathers, in time past, distinguished right and wrong plainly enough, and it is our wisdom to submit to be taught by them.&#8221;</p><footer>Refusing the king Candaules, who wished him to see the queen unclothed (Herodotus, Histories, book 1) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_1" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
